faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: router6’s host firewall (iptables), specifically the FORWARD chain policy applied to its WAN-facing interface eth2 (10.50.1.2).

Mechanism: End-to-end routing tables look fine (static kernel routes for 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 are present across routers 0–3, and router6 has the subnets directly connected). However, traffic arriving at router6 from upstream must be forwarded from eth2 to the LAN interfaces (eth0/eth1). On router6 the FORWARD chain contains blanket drops for all traffic with -i eth2, except a narrow TCP 80 exception, so return traffic to “Location C” hosts is black-holed at router6. This makes the remote site appear to “come and go” even though routes remain installed.

Localization: router6, interface eth2. Evidence:
- Traceroute from router0 to 172.18.51.15 reaches 10.50.1.2 (router6) then stalls. 
- router6 can ping 172.18.51.15 locally; ARP entries for 172.18.51.x are present.
- iptables on router6: “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP”, “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP”, “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP”.

Root cause: Misconfigured iptables on router6 drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, unintentionally blocking inter-site transit to Location C.

Corrective fix: Remove/adjust the FORWARD drops on router6. Minimal remediation:
- iptables -I FORWARD 1 -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
- iptables -I FORWARD 1 -i eth2 -o eth0 -j ACCEPT
- iptables -I FORWARD 1 -i eth2 -o eth1 -j ACCEPT
- Then delete the blanket “-i eth2 … DROP” rules or move them below allows. Persist the corrected rules. After this, inter-site pings to 172.18.51.0/24 and 172.18.50.0/24 will succeed consistently. 
=== END ===